<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cassava</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cassava, also called manioc, is a starchy root crop native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. Carried across the Atlantic as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian Exchange</a> promoted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and other colonial powers, it became a staple food in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a> it supplemented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and helped support population and surplus production.</p></div>
